{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"can"}]},{"type":"text","value":" be useful for educational purposes — helping enforce understanding our APIs — it's not ideal in production use cases."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide covers the patterns that work well in Lit:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Building reusable field UI as custom elements that accept the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" as a property."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Splitting big forms across multiple custom elements while keeping the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" property fully typed via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reusable-field-components-with-anyfieldapi","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reusable field components with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFieldApi"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most direct way to share field UI across multiple forms in Lit is to write a custom element that accepts the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" instance as a property. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" type from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/lit-form"},{"type":"text","value":" gives you a \"this is some field, I don't care about the exact generics\" type that's perfect for that property."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the field is a property of the custom element rather than something the element owns, the host needs to subscribe to the field's store so it re-renders when the field's value or metadata change. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackStoreSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" reactive controller from "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/store/latest/docs/framework/lit/quick-start","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/lit-store"}]},{"type":"text","value":" does exactly that."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// text-field.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport type { AnyFieldApi } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\n@customElement('text-field')\nexport class TextField extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  field!: AnyFieldApi\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  label = ''\n\n  // Re-render whenever this field's store updates.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.field?.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <label>\n        <div>${this.label}</div>\n        <input\n          .value=${String(this.field.state.value ?? '')}\n          @blur=${() => this.field.handleBlur()}\n          @input=${(e: Event) =>\n            this.field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n        />\n      </label>\n      ${this.field.state.meta.isTouched && this.field.state.meta.errors.length\n        ? html`<div style=\"color: red\">\n            ${this.field.state.meta.errors.join(', ')}\n          </div>`\n        : ''}\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it inside the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" directive's render callback by passing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" instance as a property:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nexport class AppForm extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, {\n    defaultValues: { firstName: '', lastName: '' },\n  })\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'lastName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Last Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter inside the render callback remains fully typed against the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" you passed, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field.state.value"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field.handleChange"},{"type":"text","value":" are still type-checked at the call site. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<text-field>"},{"type":"text","value":" itself uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" internally because it has to accept any field shape."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your reusable component only ever wraps fields of a specific value type (for example, only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" fields), you can narrow the property type with the generic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FieldApi<...>"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" — but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFieldApi"},{"type":"text","value":" is the easiest option to start with and matches how the directive is exposed in render callbacks elsewhere."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackStoreSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts an optional second argument to scope what triggers a re-render — for example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(snapshot) => snapshot.meta.errors"},{"type":"text","value":". Passing nothing re-renders on any change to the field's store, which is the simplest default."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes forms get very large. To keep things manageable, you can break a form across multiple custom elements that each receive the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackFormController"},{"type":"text","value":" as a property."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The challenge is typing that property correctly. Writing the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackFormController<…>"},{"type":"text","value":" generics by hand is verbose and error-prone, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/lit-form"},{"type":"text","value":" provides a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" helper."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" is a type-only utility — it does no work at runtime — that takes the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FormOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" you'd pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"new TanStackFormController(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns a value whose "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" matches the controller that those options would produce."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then derive the property type for a child custom element from those shared options. As with reusable field elements, the child element receives the controller as a property and won't re-render automatically when the form's state changes — wire it up with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackStoreSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.api.store"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// child-form.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst formType = getFormType(peopleFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('child-form')\nexport class ChildForm extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof formType\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  title = 'Child Form'\n\n  // Re-render when the form's state changes.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <p>${this.title}</p>\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And use it from the parent element by passing the controller as a property:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './child-form.js'\n\n@customElement('app-root')\nexport class AppRoot extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, peopleFormOpts)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`<child-form .form=${this.form} title=\"Testing\"></child-form>`\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The child element gets a fully typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" property — including all of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" directives — without having to spell out the controller's generics by hand or maintain a hand-written type alias."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" only carries types; "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"never"}]},{"type":"text","value":" call its return value at runtime. Use it as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof getFormType(opts)"},{"type":"text","value":" (or assign to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"const"},{"type":"text","value":" and use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof"},{"type":"text","value":") and pass the actual controller instance from the parent element via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".form"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-across-multiple-forms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reusing groups of fields across multiple forms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same pattern works for sharing a group of related fields (for example, a password + confirm-password pair) across forms. Define a small custom element that takes the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" as a property typed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" — keyed against just the slice of form data the group needs — and renders the relevant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.field(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" calls."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// password-fields.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { formOptions, getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst passwordFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n})\n\nconst passwordFormType = getFormType(passwordFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('password-fields')\nexport class PasswordFields extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof passwordFormType\n\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'password' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        {\n          name: 'confirm_password',\n          validators: {\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) =>\n              value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')\n                ? 'Passwords do not match'\n                : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Confirm Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host form just needs to include the same fields in its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" (TypeScript will check that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" property assigned via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".form=${this.form}"},{"type":"text","value":" is structurally compatible with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"passwordFormType"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"reusable-field-components-with-anyfieldapi","text":"Reusable field components with AnyFieldApi","level":2},{"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","text":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces","level":2},{"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-across-multiple-forms","text":"Reusing groups of fields across multiple forms","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes — helping enforce understanding our APIs — it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nThis guide covers the patterns that work well in Lit:\n\n- Building reusable field UI as custom elements that accept the `FieldApi` as a property.\n- Splitting big forms across multiple custom elements while keeping the `form` property fully typed via `getFormType`.\n\n## Reusable field components with `AnyFieldApi`\n\nThe most direct way to share field UI across multiple forms in Lit is to write a custom element that accepts the `FieldApi` instance as a property. The `AnyFieldApi` type from `@tanstack/lit-form` gives you a \"this is some field, I don't care about the exact generics\" type that's perfect for that property.\n\nBecause the field is a property of the custom element rather than something the element owns, the host needs to subscribe to the field's store so it re-renders when the field's value or metadata change. The `TanStackStoreSelector` reactive controller from [`@tanstack/lit-store`](https://tanstack.com/store/latest/docs/framework/lit/quick-start) does exactly that.\n\n```ts\n// text-field.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport type { AnyFieldApi } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\n@customElement('text-field')\nexport class TextField extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  field!: AnyFieldApi\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  label = ''\n\n  // Re-render whenever this field's store updates.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.field?.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <label>\n        <div>${this.label}</div>\n        <input\n          .value=${String(this.field.state.value ?? '')}\n          @blur=${() => this.field.handleBlur()}\n          @input=${(e: Event) =>\n            this.field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n        />\n      </label>\n      ${this.field.state.meta.isTouched && this.field.state.meta.errors.length\n        ? html`<div style=\"color: red\">\n            ${this.field.state.meta.errors.join(', ')}\n          </div>`\n        : ''}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse it inside the `field` directive's render callback by passing the `field` instance as a property:\n\n```ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nexport class AppForm extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, {\n    defaultValues: { firstName: '', lastName: '' },\n  })\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'lastName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Last Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe `field` parameter inside the render callback remains fully typed against the `name` you passed, so `field.state.value` and `field.handleChange` are still type-checked at the call site. `<text-field>` itself uses `AnyFieldApi` internally because it has to accept any field shape.\n\n> If your reusable component only ever wraps fields of a specific value type (for example, only `string` fields), you can narrow the property type with the generic `FieldApi<...>` instead of `AnyFieldApi` — but `AnyFieldApi` is the easiest option to start with and matches how the directive is exposed in render callbacks elsewhere.\n\n> `TanStackStoreSelector` accepts an optional second argument to scope what triggers a re-render — for example, `(snapshot) => snapshot.meta.errors`. Passing nothing re-renders on any change to the field's store, which is the simplest default.\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large. To keep things manageable, you can break a form across multiple custom elements that each receive the `TanStackFormController` as a property.\n\nThe challenge is typing that property correctly. Writing the full `TanStackFormController<…>` generics by hand is verbose and error-prone, so `@tanstack/lit-form` provides a `getFormType` helper.\n\n`getFormType` is a type-only utility — it does no work at runtime — that takes the same `FormOptions` you'd pass to `new TanStackFormController(...)` and returns a value whose **type** matches the controller that those options would produce.\n\n```ts\n// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThen derive the property type for a child custom element from those shared options. As with reusable field elements, the child element receives the controller as a property and won't re-render automatically when the form's state changes — wire it up with `TanStackStoreSelector` against `form.api.store`:\n\n```ts\n// child-form.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst formType = getFormType(peopleFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('child-form')\nexport class ChildForm extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof formType\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  title = 'Child Form'\n\n  // Re-render when the form's state changes.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <p>${this.title}</p>\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd use it from the parent element by passing the controller as a property:\n\n```ts\n// app.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './child-form.js'\n\n@customElement('app-root')\nexport class AppRoot extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, peopleFormOpts)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`<child-form .form=${this.form} title=\"Testing\"></child-form>`\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe child element gets a fully typed `form` property — including all of the `field` and `group` directives — without having to spell out the controller's generics by hand or maintain a hand-written type alias.\n\n> `getFormType` only carries types; **never** call its return value at runtime. Use it as `typeof getFormType(opts)` (or assign to a `const` and use `typeof`) and pass the actual controller instance from the parent element via the `.form` property.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields across multiple forms\n\nThe same pattern works for sharing a group of related fields (for example, a password + confirm-password pair) across forms. Define a small custom element that takes the `form` as a property typed with `getFormType` — keyed against just the slice of form data the group needs — and renders the relevant `form.field(...)` calls.\n\n```ts\n// password-fields.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { formOptions, getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst passwordFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n})\n\nconst passwordFormType = getFormType(passwordFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('password-fields')\nexport class PasswordFields extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof passwordFormType\n\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'password' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        {\n          name: 'confirm_password',\n          validators: {\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) =>\n              value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')\n                ? 'Passwords do not match'\n                : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Confirm Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe host form just needs to include the same fields in its own `defaultValues` (TypeScript will check that the `form` property assigned via `.form=${this.form}` is structurally compatible with `passwordFormType`).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: form-composition\ntitle: Form Composition\n---\n\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes — helping enforce understanding our APIs — it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nThis guide covers the patterns that work well in Lit:\n\n- Building reusable field UI as custom elements that accept the `FieldApi` as a property.\n- Splitting big forms across multiple custom elements while keeping the `form` property fully typed via `getFormType`.\n\n## Reusable field components with `AnyFieldApi`\n\nThe most direct way to share field UI across multiple forms in Lit is to write a custom element that accepts the `FieldApi` instance as a property. The `AnyFieldApi` type from `@tanstack/lit-form` gives you a \"this is some field, I don't care about the exact generics\" type that's perfect for that property.\n\nBecause the field is a property of the custom element rather than something the element owns, the host needs to subscribe to the field's store so it re-renders when the field's value or metadata change. The `TanStackStoreSelector` reactive controller from [`@tanstack/lit-store`](https://tanstack.com/store/latest/docs/framework/lit/quick-start) does exactly that.\n\n```ts\n// text-field.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport type { AnyFieldApi } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\n@customElement('text-field')\nexport class TextField extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  field!: AnyFieldApi\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  label = ''\n\n  // Re-render whenever this field's store updates.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.field?.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <label>\n        <div>${this.label}</div>\n        <input\n          .value=${String(this.field.state.value ?? '')}\n          @blur=${() => this.field.handleBlur()}\n          @input=${(e: Event) =>\n            this.field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n        />\n      </label>\n      ${this.field.state.meta.isTouched && this.field.state.meta.errors.length\n        ? html`<div style=\"color: red\">\n            ${this.field.state.meta.errors.join(', ')}\n          </div>`\n        : ''}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse it inside the `field` directive's render callback by passing the `field` instance as a property:\n\n```ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nexport class AppForm extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, {\n    defaultValues: { firstName: '', lastName: '' },\n  })\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'lastName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Last Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe `field` parameter inside the render callback remains fully typed against the `name` you passed, so `field.state.value` and `field.handleChange` are still type-checked at the call site. `<text-field>` itself uses `AnyFieldApi` internally because it has to accept any field shape.\n\n> If your reusable component only ever wraps fields of a specific value type (for example, only `string` fields), you can narrow the property type with the generic `FieldApi<...>` instead of `AnyFieldApi` — but `AnyFieldApi` is the easiest option to start with and matches how the directive is exposed in render callbacks elsewhere.\n\n> `TanStackStoreSelector` accepts an optional second argument to scope what triggers a re-render — for example, `(snapshot) => snapshot.meta.errors`. Passing nothing re-renders on any change to the field's store, which is the simplest default.\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large. To keep things manageable, you can break a form across multiple custom elements that each receive the `TanStackFormController` as a property.\n\nThe challenge is typing that property correctly. Writing the full `TanStackFormController<…>` generics by hand is verbose and error-prone, so `@tanstack/lit-form` provides a `getFormType` helper.\n\n`getFormType` is a type-only utility — it does no work at runtime — that takes the same `FormOptions` you'd pass to `new TanStackFormController(...)` and returns a value whose **type** matches the controller that those options would produce.\n\n```ts\n// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThen derive the property type for a child custom element from those shared options. As with reusable field elements, the child element receives the controller as a property and won't re-render automatically when the form's state changes — wire it up with `TanStackStoreSelector` against `form.api.store`:\n\n```ts\n// child-form.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst formType = getFormType(peopleFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('child-form')\nexport class ChildForm extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof formType\n\n  @property({ type: String })\n  title = 'Child Form'\n\n  // Re-render when the form's state changes.\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      <p>${this.title}</p>\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'firstName' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"First Name\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd use it from the parent element by passing the controller as a property:\n\n```ts\n// app.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackFormController } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\nimport './child-form.js'\n\n@customElement('app-root')\nexport class AppRoot extends LitElement {\n  form = new TanStackFormController(this, peopleFormOpts)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`<child-form .form=${this.form} title=\"Testing\"></child-form>`\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe child element gets a fully typed `form` property — including all of the `field` and `group` directives — without having to spell out the controller's generics by hand or maintain a hand-written type alias.\n\n> `getFormType` only carries types; **never** call its return value at runtime. Use it as `typeof getFormType(opts)` (or assign to a `const` and use `typeof`) and pass the actual controller instance from the parent element via the `.form` property.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields across multiple forms\n\nThe same pattern works for sharing a group of related fields (for example, a password + confirm-password pair) across forms. Define a small custom element that takes the `form` as a property typed with `getFormType` — keyed against just the slice of form data the group needs — and renders the relevant `form.field(...)` calls.\n\n```ts\n// password-fields.ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, property } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { TanStackStoreSelector } from '@tanstack/lit-store'\nimport { formOptions, getFormType } from '@tanstack/lit-form'\nimport './text-field.js'\n\nconst passwordFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n})\n\nconst passwordFormType = getFormType(passwordFormOpts)\n\n@customElement('password-fields')\nexport class PasswordFields extends LitElement {\n  @property({ attribute: false })\n  form!: typeof passwordFormType\n\n  _selector = new TanStackStoreSelector(this, () => this.form?.api.store)\n\n  render() {\n    return html`\n      ${this.form.field(\n        { name: 'password' },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n      ${this.form.field(\n        {\n          name: 'confirm_password',\n          validators: {\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) =>\n              value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')\n                ? 'Passwords do not match'\n                : undefined,\n          },\n        },\n        (field) => html`\n          <text-field label=\"Confirm Password\" .field=${field}></text-field>\n        `,\n      )}\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe host form just needs to include the same fields in its own `defaultValues` (TypeScript will check that the `form` property assigned via `.form=${this.form}` is structurally compatible with `passwordFormType`).\n"}
